XML Lab Activity – Reflection

In this laboratory activity, I learned how to create and validate a basic XML document. I applied the basic rules of XML such as using one root element, properly opening and closing tags, maintaining the correct nesting of elements, and using matching tag names. I also learned that XML is case-sensitive, so the opening and closing tags must be written correctly.

XML needs a single root element because it provides one main starting point for the entire document and organizes all other elements into a proper structure. This makes the XML document easier for computers and applications to read and process.

XML ensures interoperability because it uses a standardized text-based structure that can be understood by different operating systems, programming languages, and applications. Because of this, data can be stored, transferred, and processed between different systems without depending on a specific software or platform.

Overall, validating the XML helped me understand the importance of following the correct syntax and structure. It also showed me that even small errors in XML tags can cause validation problems.
